isolation) vs P1 (operator topology) provide natural sequencing without formal tiers.\n\nNow epic bodies. Let me write them carefully — self-contained, no sibling IDs.\n\n**E001 body:**\n\n# Investigation: Workspace resolution initialization coupling in the MLflow Kubernetes auth plugin\n\n## Description\nResolve whether workspace resolution in the mlflow-kubernetes-plugins auth plugin (the kubernetes-auth workspace provider registered through the mlflow.workspace_provider entry point group) can be initialized and exercised in a server context where tracking stores and model registry stores are not initialized. This is the strategy's principal unknown: the strategy assumes the workspace store (kubernetes://) provides experiment-to-workspace mapping accessible without tracking stores, and the plugin may instead couple workspace resolution to the full tracking server initialization path. The outcome determines the scope of the plugin extension work: verification only, or a refactoring that factors workspace resolution into an independent initialization path.\n\n## Scope\n- Trace the plugin's workspace provider initialization path: entry point registration, WorkspaceStoreRegistry loading, and the dependencies each step requires\n- Identify whether workspace resolution (experiment-to-workspace lookup) reads or requires tracking store or model registry store state\n- Determine whether artifact-request path context (experiment ID / run ID) is sufficient input for the plugin's workspace resolution without tracking store queries\n- If coupling exists, enumerate the refactoring required to factor workspace resolution into an independent initialization path within the plugin\n- Deliver a findings report with a pass/fail determination and a scope recommendation for the plugin extension work\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- The plugin's initialization dependency graph is documented, enumerating every store or client workspace resolution touches\n- A determination is recorded: workspace resolution functions without tracking store initialization (pass) or is coupled to it (fail), with the coupling points identified\n- If coupled, the refactoring approach (independent initialization path) is specified with the affected plugin modules listed\n- The findings are written up so the plugin extension work can be scoped directly from them\n\n## HLR Traceability\n- Resolves the open question \"Does the workspace store initialization in mlflow-kubernetes-plugins depend on tracking store availability?\" and de-risks the P1 HLR \"Auth plugin workspace resolution functions correctly in the artifacts-only context without tracking store initialization\", which enables the P0 workspace-validation HLRs\n\nHmm — \"no cross-references to sibling epics\" — mentioning HLRs is fine. OK.\n\n**E002 body:**\n\n# Investigation: Artifact traffic routing design for the split-server MLflow topology\n\n## Description\nDetermine how the MLflow operator should configure artifact traffic routing from the main tracking deployment to a dedicated artifacts-only instance in the split-server topology. The strategy leaves this open: the answer affects the HTTPRoute design and determines whether the tracking server needs a configuration change to delegate artifact operations. The outcome selects the routing mechanism the operator implementation will build.\n\n## Scope\n- Evaluate routing options: gateway-level routing of artifact API traffic to the dedicated instance (e.g., path-based HTTPRoute rules) versus configuring the main tracking deployment to delegate artifact operations to the dedicated instance\n- Determine whether the main tracking server requires a configuration change to delegate artifact operations, or whether routing can be handled entirely at the gateway layer\n- Assess implications for the dedicated instance's HTTPRoute design (host, path matching, and the operator's conditional CRD discovery pattern for Gateway API)\n- Verify kube-rbac-proxy sidecar authentication behavior is identical whichever routing mechanism is selected (authentication at ingress is unchanged)\n- Deliver a design decision documenting the selected mechanism and its constraints\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- A routing design decision is documented, selecting the mechanism for routing artifact traffic from the main tracking deployment to the dedicated artifacts-only instance\n- The decision states explicitly whether the tracking server needs a configuration change to delegate artifact operations, and what that change is\n- The HTTPRoute design for the dedicated instance (and any main-deployment routing configuration) is specified at the level the operator implementation can build against\n- The decision is consistent with the operator's existing conditional Gateway API CRD discovery pattern\n\n## HLR Traceability\n- Resolves the open question \"How should the operator configure artifact traffic routing from the main tracking server to the dedicated artifacts-only instance?\", gating the P1 HLRs \"MLflow operator deploys a dedicated artifacts-only instance as a separate Deployment with its own Service and HTTPRoute\" and \"Main tracking deployment routes artifact traffic to the dedicated artifacts-only instance\"\n\n**E003 body:**\n\n# Workspace-aware artifacts-only serving in the MLflow tracking server\n\n## Description\nMake the MLflow tracking server's `--artifacts-only` mode composable with `--enable-workspaces` so artifact serving is validated against workspace membership. When both flags are set, the server requires an explicit `--workspace-store-uri`, initializes only the workspace provider needed to validate artifact requests, and leaves tracking stores, model registry stores, and non-artifact API endpoints uninitialized/disabled. Per the Staff Engineer input, no new server flag is introduced — the workspace-aware behavior emerges from combining the two existing flags.\n\n## Scope\n- Compose `--artifacts-only` with `--enable-workspaces`: require an explicit `--workspace-store-uri` when both are set; fail to start with a clear error if it is missing\n- Initialize only the workspace provider in artifacts-only mode; keep tracking and model registry stores uninitialized and non-artifact API endpoints disabled\n- Add workspace validation to artifact request handling: resolve the artifact path to its parent experiment (artifacts stored under experiment-scoped paths, e.g. `{experiment_id}/{run_id}/artifacts/`), look up the experiment's workspace assignment via the workspace store, and delegate authorization to the existing auth plugin infrastructure\n- Deny requests from users without workspace membership with HTTP 403 and an error message indicating insufficient workspace permissions\n- Fail closed: if the workspace store is unreachable while workspace-aware mode is active, deny artifact requests (HTTP 503 indicating workspace validation unavailable) rather than serving without validation\n- Preserve current behavior when either flag is absent: `--artifacts-only` without `--enable-workspaces` serves artifacts without workspace validation; deployments without workspace flags are unaffected\n- Implement short-TTL caching of workspace membership decisions, consistent with caching patterns already used in the tracking server's auth path, to bound per-request latency of workspace resolution\n- Scope initial path resolution to the S3 artifact store (the standard RHOAI configuration) and document limitations for non-standard artifact roots\n- Measure and report the per-request latency overhead of workspace validation\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- Given the artifacts-only server started with `--artifacts-only --enable-workspaces --workspace-store-uri kubernetes://`, a user who is a member of workspace A requesting an artifact of an experiment in workspace A is served (HTTP 200, correct content)\n- Given the same deployment, a user who is a member of workspace A but not workspace B requesting an artifact of an experiment in workspace B is denied (HTTP 403, error message indicating insufficient workspace permissions)\n- Given `--artifacts-only` without `--enable-workspaces`, any authenticated user is served without workspace validation (current behavior preserved)\n- Given workspace-aware mode active and the workspace store (Kubernetes API) unreachable, artifact requests are denied (HTTP 503, workspace validation unavailable) — fail-closed\n- Given `--enable-workspaces` set without `--workspace-store-uri` in artifacts-only mode, the server fails to start with a clear error\n- Single-tenant deployments and configurations without workspace flags retain current behavior (regression-covered)\n- Per-request workspace validation overhead is measured and reported against the latency budget agreed with PM/Engineering\n- Limitations of artifact path resolution for non-standard artifact roots are documented\n\n## HLR Traceability\n- [P0] `--artifacts-only` can be combined with `--enable-workspaces` and `--workspace-store-uri` to enable workspace-validated artifact serving\n- [P0] Artifact requests through the workspace-aware artifacts-only server are validated against workspace membership; requests from users without membership are denied with HTTP 403\n- [P0] Workspace-aware artifact serving is fail-closed when the workspace store is unreachable\n- [P0] Single-tenant deployments and configurations without `--enable-workspaces` retain current behavior\n\n**E004 body:**\n\n# Artifact-context workspace resolution in the MLflow Kubernetes plugins\n\n## Description\nVerify and extend the mlflow-kubernetes-plugins auth plugin so workspace resolution (experiment-to-workspace mapping through the workspace provider registered via the `mlflow.workspace_provider` entry point group) functions correctly in the artifacts-only server context, where tracking stores and model registry stores are not initialized. If the workspace resolution is coupled to the full tracking server initialization path, factor it into an independent initialization path within the plugin.\n\n## Scope\n- Verify the plugin's workspace resolution works when initialized in the artifacts-only context (workspace store only, no tracking/model registry stores)\n- If coupled to tracking store availability, refactor workspace resolution into an independent initialization path within the plugin\n- Ensure workspace resolution is callable with artifact-request context (experiment ID resolved from the artifact path) and returns the workspace assignment the server's authorization check needs\n- Keep the kube-rbac-proxy sidecar and `--app-name kubernetes-auth` setup unchanged — authentication at ingress works identically for both server types\n- Add test coverage for workspace resolution in the artifacts-only context (member and non-member scenarios)\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- Workspace resolution (experiment-to-workspace lookup) is demonstrated to function in a server context with no tracking store or model registry store initialization\n- If coupling was found, workspace resolution is factored into an independent initialization path and the refactoring is covered by tests\n- Workspace resolution resolves artifact-request context to a workspace assignment with correct member/non-member results in tests\n- No change is required to the kube-rbac-proxy sidecar or `--app-name kubernetes-auth` setup\n\n## HLR Traceability\n- [P1] Auth plugin workspace resolution functions correctly in the artifacts-only context without tracking store initialization (this HLR is labeled P1 in the strategy; the epic's priority is raised to P0 by inheritance because workspace resolution enables the P0 workspace-validation requirements)\n\n**E005 body:**\n\n# Split-server artifact serving topology in the MLflow operator\n\n## Description\nExtend the MLflow operator to deploy a dedicated artifacts-only MLflow instance as a separate Deployment with its own Service and HTTPRoute, and to route artifact traffic from the main tracking deployment to that dedicated instance — a split-server topology managed declaratively through a single MLflow CR. The operator propagates workspace configuration to the artifacts-only instance using the same configuration mechanisms it already uses for the main tracking deployment.\n\n## Scope\n- New Helm chart templates (within the operator's existing Helm-in-operator pattern) for the artifacts-only Deployment, Service, and HTTPRoute\n- Propagate workspace configuration (`--enable-workspaces`, `--workspace-store-uri`, `--app-name kubernetes-auth`, `--serve-artifacts`) to the artifacts-only instance, combining `--artifacts-only` with the workspace flags — no new CR field of the form spec.artifactsOnly.workspaceAware (per Staff Engineer input, the workspace-aware behavior comes from flag composition)\n- Configure artifact traffic routing from the main tracking deployment to the dedicated instance per the selected routing design (gateway-level routing or main-deployment delegation)\n- Create the artifacts-only instance's Gateway API HTTPRoute conditionally based on runtime CRD discovery, consistent with the operator's existing pattern for optional dependencies\n- Operator validation: reject configurations where the artifacts-only instance would run without the workspace flags when workspace-aware artifact serving is intended, so misconfiguration cannot bypass workspace validation\n- Deploy the kube-rbac-proxy sidecar on the artifacts-only instance identically to the main tracking deployment\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- Given an MLflow CR with split-server artifact serving enabled, reconciliation creates a dedicated artifacts-only Deployment, Service, and HTTPRoute separate from the main tracking Deployment (distinct Deployment resources with the correct flag combinations)\n- The artifacts-only Deployment's container command runs `--artifacts-only --enable-workspaces --workspace-store-uri kubernetes:// --app-name kubernetes-auth --serve-artifacts` (verified in the rendered Deployment spec)\n- Artifact traffic from the main tracking deployment is routed to the dedicated artifacts-only instance per the selected routing design\n- The artifacts-only HTTPRoute is created only when Gateway API CRDs are discovered at runtime\n- The operator rejects invalid configurations (artifacts-only instance lacking workspace flags where workspace-aware serving is intended)\n- Deployments that do not enable the split topology are reconciled unchanged (backwards compatibility)\n- The rendered topology is verified end-to-end against a workspace-aware artifacts-only server build (flag combination supported by the server) — acceptance gate, not a build dependency\n\n## HLR Traceability\n- [P1] MLflow operator deploys a dedicated artifacts-only instance as a separate Deployment with its own Service and HTTPRoute\n- [P1] Main tracking deployment routes artifact traffic to the dedicated artifacts-only instance\n\n**E006 body:**\n\n# Decide the audit telemetry channel for workspace access denials\n\n## Description\nDecide through which telemetry channel workspace access denials in artifact serving should generate audit events: server logs (the baseline stated in the strategy's P2 requirement), OTLP telemetry, or the Kubernetes audit log. The strategy leaves this to PM/Security with technical feasibility input from the MLflow team; the decision determines the scope of the audit logging implementation.\n\n## Scope\n- Frame the decision: auditability requirements for workspace access denials (who needs to consume the events, retention, correlation)\n- Assess feasibility and cost of each channel: server log emission, OTLP export through the existing observability stack, Kubernetes audit log integration\n- Record the PM/Security decision with rationale, including whether server logs alone satisfy the requirement or an additional channel is required\n- Specify the audit event content required (identity, workspace, experiment, decision) at the level the implementation can