rving through server logs (channel per the audit channel decision; split from the server implementation because the channel decision gates its scope, though it instruments the same denial path)\n\n**E008 body:**\n\n# End-to-end integration validation of workspace-aware artifact serving\n\n## Description\nValidate the assembled workspace-aware artifact serving capability end to end across the split-server topology: the workspace-aware artifacts-only server, the plugin's workspace resolution, and the operator-deployed dedicated instance with artifact traffic routing. This is the strategy's integration testing effort (multi-tenant workspace scenarios, backwards compatibility, single-tenant regression, fail-closed edge cases, split-server routing).\n\n## Scope\n- Multi-tenant workspace scenarios through the operator-deployed topology: member access granted (200), non-member denied (403)\n- Fail-closed edge cases: workspace store unreachable → denials (503), no silent degradation of tenant isolation\n- Single-tenant regression and backwards compatibility: deployments without workspace flags and artifacts-only without workspaces retain current behavior\n- Split-server routing: artifact traffic from the main tracking deployment reaches the dedicated artifacts-only instance; misconfiguration scenarios rejected by operator validation\n- S3 artifact store path resolution end to end (standard RHOAI configuration)\n- Test results and evidence captured for the strategy's acceptance criteria\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- All multi-tenant workspace scenarios pass through the operator-deployed split topology (member 200 with correct content; non-member 403)\n- Fail-closed behavior verified: workspace store unreachability yields denials, not unvalidated serves\n- Single-tenant and no-workspace-flag regressions pass (current behavior preserved)\n- Artifact traffic routing between main tracking deployment and dedicated instance verified end to end\n- A validation report maps each strategy acceptance criterion to its observed result\n\n## HLR Traceability\n- Validation coverage for [P0] workspace-validated artifact serving, [P0] membership denial (403), [P0] fail-closed behavior, [P0] backwards compatibility, [P1] dedicated artifacts-only instance deployment, [P1] artifact traffic routing — the implementing epics own these HLRs; this epic validates them end to end\n\nNow signal files. One per epic: {ID}-E001-ai-signals.md etc. (for investigations too — same filename pattern per prompt: \"Write the signal rationale to a separate file artifacts/epic-tasks/{ID}-ENNN-ai-signals.md (one per epic), for whichever signal set you used.\")\n\nNow the decomposition summary body. Sections: Epic List, Dependency DAG (Mermaid), DAG Justification, HLR Traceability Matrix, Health Warnings, Tiered Delivery. Plus I'll add: Triage result (not triaged — proceeded to full decomposition), Staff Engineer Input handling, component mapping notes, open question disposition. The prompt lists required sections; additional sections allowed? It says \"with these sections\" — I'll include the required ones plus a few extra (Open Question Disposition, Component Mapping, Staff Engineer Input) — should be fine and improves traceability. Hmm, \"write the body content (no frontmatter delimiters) with these sections\" — minimum. I'll add extras.\n\nEpic List table: ID, title, type, team, priority.\n\n| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n| E001 | Investigate workspace resolution initialization coupling in the MLflow Kubernetes auth plugin | Investigation | MLflow Team | P0 |\n| E002 | Investigate artifact traffic routing design for the split-server MLflow topology | Investigation | MLflow Team | P1 |\n| E003 | Workspace-aware artifacts-only serving in the MLflow tracking server | Implementation | MLflow Team | P0 |\n| E004 | Artifact-context workspace resolution in the MLflow Kubernetes plugins | Implementation | MLflow Team | P0 |\n| E005 | Split-server artifact serving topology in the MLflow operator | Implementation | MLflow Team | P1 |\n| E006 | Decide the audit telemetry channel for workspace access denials | Investigation | MLflow Team | P2 |\n| E007 | Audit logging for workspace access denials in artifact serving | Implementation | MLflow Team | P2 |\n| E008 | End-to-end integration validation of workspace-aware artifact serving | Implementation | MLflow Team | P1 |\n\nDAG Justification table with edges + rules + non-edges.\n\nNow write everything. Order: summary body first (Step 8a), then frontmatter set, then epic files + frontmatter, signal files, batch-read, validate, then decompose_complete=true.\n\nLet me now write the decomposition summary file. No frontmatter delimiters — just body; the script adds frontmatter. Wait — does frontmatter.py set work on a file with no frontmatter? Presumably it creates it. Let me write body then run set.\n\nOne concern: does the summary file need specific section headings for downstream? Prompt lists: Epic List, Dependency DAG, DAG Justification, HLR Traceability Matrix, Health Warnings, Tiered Delivery. I'll use exactly those headings plus extras.\n\nLet me write the files now. I'll use Write tool. Mind: bash working dir — scripts are at scripts/ relative to working dir /private/var/folders/.../epic-RHAISTRAT-8009-g5n6ydpn. Bash cwd resets between calls but starts at primary working directory. Use absolute paths or rely on cwd. The note says cwd resets between calls to the primary working directory — which IS the workspace root. So relative paths like scripts/frontmatter.py and artifacts/... work.\n\nLet me write the summary body.","signature":"7c205fcf790f4071a29715b2514592a4"}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":73454,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b34a512e1b7fc615","agent_id":"a8c8fab44d11c6743","session_id":"1dcc8d9d-3485-4c95-9a3d-f9307d0024ef","uuid":"9d6ded47-36f6-49b6-9548-2c1512