2, gating E005 (`rewrite`) | Answer changes the operator epic's approach (HTTPRoute design; whether the tracking server needs a configuration change). |\n| Audit telemetry channel for access denials (OQ4) | Investigation E006, gating E007 (`rewrite`) | Answer changes what the audit implementation builds. Routes to a person (PM/Security governance decision). |\n| Acceptable latency overhead / caching at launch (OQ1) | AC + health warning (HW-3) on E003 | Implementation proceeds identically either way: the strategy's risk mitigation already prescribes short-TTL caching of workspace membership decisions, and no latency target exists to investigate against — the PM budget only determines whether prescribed caching could later be simplified. Captured as a measured-and-reported AC plus a warning; not a structural unknown. |\n\nAssumptions marked \"needs validation\": the flag composability, Helm-chart extensibility, and single-tenant behavior assumptions are the implementing epics' own work (ACs); the artifact-path-sufficiency assumption is bounded by the S3-scoping risk mitigation (AC + documented limitations on E003); the workspace-store coupling assumption is E001.\n\n## Health Warnings\n\n- **HW-1 (priority inversion flag)**: The strategy labels the auth-plugin HLR P1, but plugin workspace resolution enables the P0 membership-validation and fail-closed HLRs; priority inheritance raises E004 (and E001) to P0. Human verification requested: confirm the plugin work is genuinely P0, or re-scope the P0 validation HLRs.\n- **HW-2 (validation epic priority)**: E008 (integration validation) is P1, not P0: the P0 server capabilities are acceptance-gated within E003's own ACs, and E008's critical path runs through the P1 operator topology. If PM wants the integration report treated as P0 acceptance evidence, note that E005 would then inherit P0 by the priority-inheritance rule.\n- **HW-3 (undefined latency budget)**: No latency target is specified in the RFE or architecture context (OQ1, owner PM/Engineering). Short-TTL caching is implemented per the risk mitigation regardless; E003 carries a \"per-request overhead measured and reported\" AC. PM decision pending on whether caching is launch-blocking or deferrable.\n- **HW-4 (pending process gates)**: Architecture Review, ODH/RHOAI Build Onboarding, Licence Validation, and other prerequisites are all \"Pending human review\" at the strategy level. Both target repos already run Konflux pipelines (per mlflow.md / mlflow-operator.md recent changes), so no onboarding epics were generated; the pending human reviews remain a strategy-level gate outside epic scope.\n- **HW-5 (effort overage with re-split trigger)**: E005 (operator split topology) is estimated at 2–3 sprints, above the ~1-sprint guideline. Kept as one epic because it is a single logical change over one surface (the Helm-in-operator chart and reconciler): splitting instance deployment from traffic routing would double-touch the same templates and fragment the chart work. **Re-split trigger**: re-split E005 if the routing design investigation shows main-deployment delegation is independently schedulable, or if chart-template volume exceeds the estimate during implementation.\n- **HW-6 (scope bound)**: Artifact path resolution is scoped to the S3 artifact store (standard RHOAI configuration, `{experiment_id}/{run_id}/artifacts/` structure) per the risk mitigation; limitations for non-standard artifact roots are documented, not implemented.\n\n## Tiered Delivery\n\nNot applicable. All dependencies are internal and already exist (workspace store, auth plugin, Gateway API with conditional CRD discovery, kube-rbac-proxy); no external dependency has uncertain timing, so no Tier 1/Tier 2 split is needed. The P0 (server-side isolation) vs. P1 (operator topology) vs. P2 (audit) priorities provide natural sequencing without formal tiers.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":73454,