{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b57f40846c8f36be","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-8009-E001\n3\ttitle: Investigate workspace resolution initialization coupling in the MLflow Kubernetes\n4\t  auth plugin\n5\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8009\n6\tcomponent: MLflow\n7\tteam: MLflow Team\n8\ttype: Investigation\n9\tpriority: P0\n10\tdependencies: []\n11\tinvestigation_signals:\n12\t  question_specificity: 1\n13\t  source_accessibility: 1\n14\t  local_runnability: 1\n15\t  cluster_hardware_dependence: -1\n16\t  human_judgment_required: 0\n17\timplementation_type: null\n18\tai_signals: null\n19\tai_implementability: Medium\n20\tai_implementability_score: 2\n21\tjira_key: null\n22\tbranch: null\n23\tgated_by: null\n24\tgate_failure_impact: null\n25\t---\n26\t# Investigate workspace resolution initialization coupling in the MLflow Kubernetes auth plugin\n27\t\n28\t## Description\n29\t\n30\tResolve whether workspace resolution in the mlflow-kubernetes-plugins auth plugin — the workspace provider registered through the `mlflow.workspace_provider` entry point group and loaded by `WorkspaceStoreRegistry` — can be initialized and exercised in a server context where tracking stores and model registry stores are not initialized. This is the strategy's principal unknown: the strategy assumes the workspace store (`kubernetes://`) provides experiment-to-workspace mapping accessible without tracking stores, while the risk register warns the plugin's workspace resolution may be coupled to the full tracking server initialization path. The outcome determines the scope of the plugin extension work: verification only, or a refactoring that factors workspace resolution into an independent initialization path within the plugin.\n31\t\n32\t## Scope\n33\t\n34\t- Trace the plugin's workspace provider initialization path: entry point registration, `WorkspaceStoreRegistry` loading, and every store, client, or configuration object each step requires\n35\t- Identify whether workspace resolution (experiment-to-workspace lookup) reads or requires tracking store or model registry store state\n36\t- Determine whether artifact-request context (experiment ID resolved from the artifact path) is sufficient input for the plugin's workspace resolution without tracking store queries\n37\t- If coupling exists, enumerate the refactoring needed to factor workspace resolution into an independent initialization path within the plugin, listing the affected plugin modules\n38\t- Deliver a findings report with a clear determination and a scope recommendation for the plugin extension work\n39\t\n40\t## Acceptance Criteria\n41\t\n42\t- The plugin's initialization dependency graph is documented, enumerating every store, client, or service workspace resolution touches\n43\t- A determination is recorded with pass/fail criteria: workspace resolution functions without tracking store initialization (pass), or is coupled to it (fail) with the coupling points identified\n44\t- If coupled, the refactoring approach for an independent initialization path is specified at the level the plugin extension work can be scoped from\n45\t- The findings are written up so the plugin extension epic's scope can be finalized directly from them\n46\t\n47\t## HLR Traceability\n48\t\n49\t- Resolves the strategy's open question \"Does the workspace store initialization in mlflow-kubernetes-plugins depend on tracking store availability?\" and the assumption \"the workspace store provides experiment-to-workspace mapping accessible without initializing tracking stores\"\n50\t- De-risks the HLR \"Auth plugin workspace resolution functions correctly in the artifacts-only context without tracking store initialization\" (labeled P1 in the strategy), which enables the P0 workspace-validation and fail-closed HLRs — hence this investigation's P0 priority by inheritance\n51\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8a1b800ebeb3e27f","agent_id":"a