{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-be9c4bb3805391cb","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8009\n3\tepic_count: 8\n4\tcritical_path_length: 4\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\ttriage: null\n7\ttriage_rationale: null\n8\t---\n9\t# Decomposition Summary: RHAISTRAT-8009 — Workspace-aware artifact serving for multi-tenant MLflow deployments\n10\t\n11\t## Triage Result\n12\t\n13\tNot triaged as below-threshold or docs-only. The strategy is Critical priority, M-L effort (4–7 sprints), and spans three architecturally distinct sub-systems (Python MLflow server, Go MLflow operator, mlflow-kubernetes-plugins package) that warrant separate PR review cycles, with multiple genuine unknowns (4 open questions, 5 assumptions marked \"needs validation\"). Full decomposition follows.\n14\t\n15\t## Staff Engineer Input Handling\n16\t\n17\tThe Staff Engineer / SME Input section is non-empty and takes precedence. All three corrections are already incorporated into the strategy's Technical Approach; no overrides were required:\n18\t\n19\t- **No new flag** — the approach combines existing `--artifacts-only` and `--enable-workspaces` flags; the strategy body contains no reference to `--artifacts-only-workspace-aware` or `spec.artifactsOnly.workspaceAware`. The server and operator epics carry this constraint explicitly in scope.\n20\t- **Operator work is a split deployment, not a config toggle** — the operator epic delivers a dedicated artifacts-only Deployment with its own Service and HTTPRoute plus artifact traffic routing from the main tracking deployment.\n21\t- **SSAR detail over-specified** — the epics state that workspace authorization reuses the existing auth plugin infrastructure without dictating Kubernetes API mechanics (no SSAR/pseudo-resource prescriptions appear in any epic scope).\n22\t\n23\t## Component Mapping\n24\t\n25\tCanonical RHAI component list (`.context/rhai-components.txt`) contains `MLflow` but not `MLflow Operator` or `mlflow-kubernetes-plugins`. Mapping applied (log for submit.py traceability):\n26\t\n27\t| Strategy component | Canonical component | Notes |\n28\t|---|---|---|\n29\t| MLflow | MLflow | Direct match |\n30\t| MLflow Operator | MLflow | Closest canonical name; operator work (Go, red-hat-data-services/mlflow-operator) is tracked under the MLflow component |\n31\t| mlflow-kubernetes-plugins | MLflow | Closest canonical name; plugin package work tracked under the MLflow component |\n32\t\n33\t`mlflow-kubernetes-plugins` has no architecture-context file of its own, but it is an existing team-owned package (v1.5.0, documented in mlflow.md as the source of the `kubernetes-auth` plugin), not a new platform component — no repo/konflux onboarding chain is warranted. Both target repos (red-hat-data-services/mlflow, red-hat-data-services/mlflow-operator) already have Konflux pipelines per architecture context, so Rules 19–22 onboarding chains do not apply.\n34\t\n35\t## Epic List\n36\t\n37\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n38\t|---|---|---|---|---|\n39\t| RHAISTRAT-8009-E001 | Investigate workspace resolution initialization coupling in the MLflow Kubernetes auth plugin | Investigation | MLflow Team | P0 |\n40\t| RHAISTRAT-8009-E002 | Investigate artifact traffic routing design for the split-server MLflow topology | Investigation | MLflow Team | P1 |\n41\t| RHAISTRAT-8009-E003 | Workspace-aware artifacts-only serving in the MLflow tracking server | Implementation | MLflow Team | P0 |\n42\t| RHAISTRAT-8009-E004 | Artifact-context workspace resolution in the MLflow Kubernetes plugins | Implementation | MLflow Team | P0 |\n43\t| RHAISTRAT-8009-E005 | Split-server artifact serving topology in the MLflow operator | Implementation | MLflow Team | P1 |\n44\t| RHAISTRAT-8009-E006 | Decide the audit telemetry channel for workspace access denials | Investigation | MLflow Team | P2 |\n45\t| RHAISTRAT-8009-E007 | Audit logging for workspace access denials in artifact serving | Implementation | MLflow Team | P2 |\n46\t| RHAISTRAT-8009-E008 | End-to-end integration validation of workspace-aware artifact serving | Implementation | MLflow Team | P1 |\n47\t\n48\t## Dependency DAG\n49\t\n50\t```mermaid\n51\tgraph TD\n52\t    E001[\"E001 Investigation: plugin workspace-resolution coupling\"] --> E004[\"E004 Plugin workspace resolution for artifacts-only context\"]\n53\t    E004 --> E003[\"E003 Server: workspace-aware artifacts-only serving\"]\n54\t    E002[\"E002 Investigation: artifact traffic routing design\"] --> E005[\"E005 Operator: split-server topology\"]\n55\t    E003 --> E008[\"E008 End-to-end integration validation\"]\n56\t    E004 --> E008\n57\t    E005 --> E008\n58\t    E003 --> E007[\"E007 Audit logging for access denials\"]\n59\t    E006[\"E006 Investigation: audit telemetry channel\"] --> E007\n60\t```\n61\t\n62\tRoots: E001, E002, E006 (all three start in parallel). Critical path: E001 → E004 → E003 → E008 (4 epics).\n63\t\n64\t## DAG Justification\n65\t\n66\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n67\t|---|---|---|\n68\t| E001 → E004 | Rule 3 (Investigation edges) | The plugin-coupling investigation determines the plugin epic's scope: verification only (not coupled) vs. refactoring workspace resolution into an independent initialization path (coupled). True gate — `rewrite`. |\n69\t| E004 → E003 | Rule 13 (artifact another epic builds against) | The plugin is a library the server loads; the server's new artifact-request validation code builds against the plugin's workspace resolution. Consuming implementation is serial after the library. |\n70\t| E002 → E005 | Rule 3 (Investigation edges) | The routing design investigation determines the operator epic's approach (gateway-level routing vs. main-deployment delegation, and whether the tracking server needs a configuration change). True gate — `rewrite`. |\n71\t| E003 → E008 | Rule 13 / integration consumption | Integration validation exercises the server capability end to end. |\n72\t| E004 → E008 | Rule 13 / integration consumption | Integration validation exercises plugin workspace resolution in the deployed topology. |\n73\t| E005 → E008 | Rule 13 / integration consumption | Split-server routing scenarios require the operator-deployed topology. |\n74\t| E003 → E007 | Rule 13 | Audit logging instruments the workspace-denial code path the server epic creates; it builds on that surface. |\n75\t| E006 → E007 | Rule 3 (Investigation edges) | The channel decision (server logs vs. OTLP vs. Kubernetes audit log) determines the audit implementation's scope. True gate — `rewrite`. |\n76\t\n77\tDeliberate **non-edges** (parallelism):\n78\t\n79\t| Pair | Rule | Rationale |\n80\t|---|---|---|\n81\t| E005 ∥ E003/E004 | Rule 13 (configuration references are AC gates) | The operator renders the flag combination into Deployment templates — a configuration reference to the server's capability, not a build-against artifact. Coupling is expressed as an AC on E005 (\"rendered topology verified against a workspace-aware artifacts-only server build\") and validated end to end in E008. |\n82\t| E002 chain ∥ E001 chain | Rule 14 (different repos/sub-systems) | Operator routing design is independent of server/plugin workspace resolution work. |\n83\t| E006 ∥ all | Rule 14 | The audit channel decision (PM/Security) blocks only the P2 audit implementation. |\n84\t| E007 ∥ E008 | Rule 15 | Audit logging (P2) does not gate integration validation of the P0/P1 capability. |\n85\t\n86\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n87\t\n88\t| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) | Notes |\n89\t|---|---|---|---|\n90\t| `--artifacts-only` can be combined with `--enable-workspaces` and `--workspace-store-uri` for workspace-validated artifact serving | P0 | E003 (implementation), E008 (validation) | |\n91\t| Artifact requests validated against workspace membership; non-members denied HTTP 403 | P0 | E003 (implementation), E004 (enabling), E008 (validation) | |\n92\t| Fail-closed: workspace store unreachable → requests denied | P0 | E003 (implementation), E008 (validation) | |\n93\t| Single-tenant / no-workspace-flag deployments retain current behavior | P0 | E003 (implementation + regression ACs), E008 (regression validation) | |\n94\t| Operator deploys dedicated artifacts-only instance (separate Deployment, Service, HTTPRoute) | P1 | E005 (implementation), E008 (validation) | |\n95\t| Main tracking deployment routes artifact traffic to the dedicated instance | P1 | E005 (implementation, per routing design), E008 (validation) | |\n96\t| Auth plugin workspace resolution functions in artifacts-only context without tracking store initialization | P1 | E004 (implementation), E001 (gating investigation) | **Raised, not bundled**: E004 is P0 by priority inheritance — it enables the P0 membership-validation and fail-closed HLRs through the server epic. The strategy labels this HLR P1; see Health Warning HW-1. |\n97\t| Audit logging for workspace access denials in artifact serving through server logs | P2 | E007 (implementation), E006 (gating investigation) | **Split from E003, not merely by priority**: the audit channel is an open question whose answer changes what the audit work builds (server logs vs. OTLP vs. Kubernetes audit log), so it cannot ride inside the P0 server epic without blocking P0 work on a P2 decision. It instruments the denial path E003 creates (shared surface caveat: the audit epic extends the denial code path rather than building a second one), and is independently deferrable. |\n98\t\n99\tPriority-split analysis: E003 bundles HLRs 1–4 (all P0, one surface — the artifact request handling path); E005 bundles HLRs 5–6 (both P1, one surface — the operator's split topology); no lower-priority HLR is incidental-bundled into a higher-priority epic.\n100\t\n101\t## Open Question Disposition\n102\t\n103\t| Open question | Disposition | Rationale |\n104\t|---|---|---|\n105\t| Plugin workspace-store initialization vs. tracking store availability (OQ3) | Investigation E001, gating E004 (`rewrite`) | Answer changes the plugin epic's scope (verify vs. refactor). Also covers assumption \"workspace store provides experiment-to-workspace mapping accessible without tracking stores\". |\n106\t| How the operator configures artifact traffic routing (OQ2) | Investigation E002, gating E005 (`rewrite`) | Answer changes the operator epic's approach (HTTPRoute design; whether the tracking server needs a configuration change). |\n107\t| Audit telemetry channel for access denials (OQ4) | Investigation E006, gating E007 (`rewrite`) | Answer changes what the audit implementation builds. Routes to a person (PM/Security governance decision). |\n108\t| Acceptable latency overhead / caching at launch (OQ1) | AC + health warning (HW-3) on E003 | Implementation proceeds identically either way: the strategy's risk mitigation already prescribes short-TTL caching of workspace membership decisions, and no latency target exists to investigate against — the PM budget only determines whether prescribed caching could later be simplified. Captured as a measured-and-reported AC plus a warning; not a structural unknown. |\n109\t\n110\tAssumptions marked \"needs validation\": the flag composability, Helm-chart extensibility, and single-tenant behavior assumptions are the implementing epics' own work (ACs); the artifact-path-sufficiency assumption is bounded by the S3-scoping risk mitigation (AC + documented limitations on E003); the workspace-store coupling assumption is E001.\n111\t\n112\t## Health Warnings\n113\t\n114\t- **HW-1 (priority inversion flag)**: The strategy labels the auth-plugin HLR P1, but plugin workspace resolution enables the P0 membership-validation and fail-closed HLRs; priority inheritance raises E004 (and E001) to P0. Human verification requested: confirm the plugin work is genuinely P0, or re-scope the P0 validation HLRs.\n115\t- **HW-2 (validation epic priority)**: E008 (integration validation) is P1, not P0: the P0 server capabilities are acceptance-gated within E003's own ACs, and E008's critical path runs through the P1 operator topology. If PM wants the integration report treated as P0 acceptance evidence, note that E005 would then inherit P0 by the priority-inheritance rule.\n116\t- **HW-3 (undefined latency budget)**: No latency target is specified in the RFE or architecture context (OQ1, owner PM/Engineering). Short-TTL caching is implemented per the risk mitigation regardless; E003 carries a \"per-request overhead measured and reported\" AC. PM decision pending on whether caching is launch-blocking or deferrable.\n117\t- **HW-4 (pending process gates)**: Architecture Review, ODH/RHOAI Build Onboarding, Licence Validation, and other prerequisites are all \"Pending human review\" at the strategy level. Both target repos already run Konflux pipelines (per mlflow.md / mlflow-operator.md recent changes), so no onboarding epics were generated; the pending human reviews remain a strategy-level gate outside epic scope.\n118\t- **HW-5 (effort overage with re-split trigger)**: E005 (operator split topology) is estimated at 2–3 sprints, above the ~1-sprint guideline. Kept as one epic because it is a single logical change over one surface (the Helm-in-operator chart and reconciler): splitting instance deployment from traffic routing would double-touch the same templates and fragment the chart work. **Re-split trigger**: re-split E005 if the routing design investigation shows main-deployment delegation is independently schedulable, or if chart-template volume exceeds the estimate during implementation.\n119\t- **HW-6 (scope bound)**: Artifact path resolution is scoped to the S3 artifact store (standard RHOAI configuration, `{experiment_id}/{run_id}/artifacts/` structure) per the risk mitigation; limitations for non-standard artifact roots are documented, not implemented.\n120\t\n121\t## Tiered Delivery\n122\t\n123\tNot applicable. All dependencies are internal and already exist (workspace store, auth plugin, Gateway API with conditional CRD discovery, kube-rbac-proxy); no external dependency has uncertain timing, so no Tier 1/Tier 2 split is needed. The P0 (server-side isolation) vs. P1 (operator topology) vs. P2 (audit) priorities provide natural sequencing without formal tiers.\n124\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-860fd22a500aa4a7","agent_id":"aaca81f619b1473ad","session_id":"1dcc8d9d-3485-4c95-9a3d-f9307d0024ef","uuid":"36f8c98d-0086-4e1e-b191-c956b5973ba0","timestamp":"2026-10-23T18:28:58.161Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-be9c4bb3805391cb","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Revise RHAISTRAT-8009 decomposition"}
