{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-81f03ede6b53af65","type":"tool_result","content":"---\nepic_id: RHAISTRAT-8009-E001\ntitle: Investigate workspace resolution initialization coupling in the MLflow Kubernetes\n  auth plugin\nparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8009\ncomponent: MLflow\nteam: MLflow Team\ntype: Investigation\npriority: P0\ndependencies: []\ninvestigation_signals:\n  question_specificity: 1\n  source_accessibility: 1\n  local_runnability: 1\n  cluster_hardware_dependence: -1\n  human_judgment_required: 0\nimplementation_type: null\nai_signals: null\nai_implementability: Medium\nai_implementability_score: 2\njira_key: null\nbranch: null\ngated_by: null\ngate_failure_impact: null\n---\n# Investigate workspace resolution initialization coupling in the MLflow Kubernetes auth plugin\n\n## Description\n\nResolve whether workspace resolution in the mlflow-kubernetes-plugins auth plugin — the workspace provider registered through the `mlflow.workspace_provider` entry point group and loaded by `WorkspaceStoreRegistry` — can be initialized and exercised in a server context where tracking stores and model registry stores are not initialized. This is the strategy's principal unknown: the strategy assumes the workspace store (`kubernetes://`) provides experiment-to-workspace mapping accessible without tracking stores, while the risk register warns the plugin's workspace resolution may be coupled to the full tracking server initialization path. The outcome determines the scope of the plugin extension work: verification only, or a refactoring that factors workspace resolution into an independent initialization path within the plugin.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Trace the plugin's workspace provider initialization path: entry point registration, `WorkspaceStoreRegistry` loading, and every store, client, or configuration object each step requires\n- Identify whether workspace resolution (experiment-to-workspace lookup) reads or requires tracking store or model registry store state\n- Determine whether artifact-request context (experiment ID resolved from the artifact path) is sufficient input for the plugin's workspace resolution without tracking store queries\n- If coupling exists, enumerate the refactoring needed to factor workspace resolution into an independent initialization path within the plugin, listing the affected plugin modules\n- Deliver a findings report with a clear determination and a scope recommendation for the plugin extension work\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- The plugin's initialization dependency graph is documented, enumerating every store, client, or service workspace resolution touches\n- A determination is recorded with pass/fail criteria: workspace resolution functions without tracking store initialization (pass), or is coupled to it (fail) with the coupling points identified\n- If coupled, the refactoring approach for an independent initialization path is specified at the level the plugin extension work can be scoped from\n- The findings are written up so the plugin extension epic's scope can be finalized directly from them\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- Resolves the strategy's open question \"Does the workspace store initialization in mlflow-kubernetes-plugins depend on tracking store availability?\" and the assumption \"the workspace store provides experiment-to-workspace mapping accessible without initializing tracking stores\"\n- De-risks the HLR \"Auth plugin workspace resolution functions correctly in the artifacts-only context without tracking store initialization\" (labeled P1 in the strategy), which enables the P0 workspace-validation and fail-closed HLRs — hence this investigation's P0 priority by inheritance\n---\nepic_id: RHAISTRAT-8009-E002\ntitle: Investigate artifact traffic routing design for the split-server MLflow topology\nparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8009\ncomponent: MLflow\nteam: MLflow Team\ntype: Investigation\npriority: P1\ndependencies: []\ninvestigation_signals:\n  question_specificity: 1\n  source_accessibility: 1\n  local_runnability: 1\n  cluster_hardware_dependence: -1\n  human_judgment_required: 0\nimplementation_type: null\nai_signals: null\nai_implementability: Medium\nai_implementability_score: 2\njira_key: null\nbranch: null\ngated_by: null\ngate_failure_impact: null\n---\n# Investigate artifact traffic routing design for the split-server MLflow topology\n\n## Description\n\nDetermine how the MLflow operator should configure artifact traffic routing from the main tracking deployment to a dedicated artifacts-only instance in the split-server topology. The strategy leaves this as an open question owned by the MLflow team: the answer affects the HTTPRoute design and determines whether the tracking server needs a configuration change to delegate artifact operations. The outcome selects the routing mechanism the operator's split-server topology implementation will build.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Evaluate routing options: gateway-level routing of artifact API traffic to the dedicated instance (for example path-based HTTPRoute rules) versus configuring the main tracking deployment to delegate artifact operations to the dedicated instance\n- Determine whether the main tracking server requires a configuration change to delegate artifact operations, or whether routing can be handled entirely at the gateway layer\n- Assess the implications for the dedicated instance's HTTPRoute design and for the operator's existing conditional Gateway API CRD discovery pattern for optional dependencies\n- Verify that kube-rbac-proxy sidecar authentication behavior is identical whichever routing mechanism is selected (authentication at ingress is unchanged for both server types, per Staff Engineer input)\n- Deliver a design decision documenting the selected routing mechanism, its constraints, and what configuration the main tracking deployment needs\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- A routing design decision is documented, selecting the mechanism for routing artifact traffic from the main tracking deployment to the dedicated artifacts-only instance\n- The decision states explicitly whether the tracking server needs a configuration change to delegate artifact operations, and what that change is\n- The HTTPRoute design for the dedicated instance (and any main-deployment routing configuration) is specified at the level the operator implementation can build against\n- The decision is consistent with the operator's existing conditional Gateway API CRD discovery pattern for optional dependencies\n- The decision confirms ingress authentication (kube-rbac-proxy sidecar, `--app-name kubernetes-auth`) is unaffected by the chosen mechanism\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- Resolves the strategy's open question \"How should the operator configure artifact traffic routing from the main tracking server to the dedicated artifacts-only instance?\"\n- Gates the HLRs \"MLflow operator deploys a dedicated artifacts-only instance as a separate Deployment with its own Service and HTTPRoute\" (P1) and \"Main tracking deployment routes artifact traffic to the dedicated artifacts-only instance\" (P1)\n---\nepic_id: RHAISTRAT-8009-E003\ntitle: Workspace-aware artifacts-only serving in the MLflow tracking server\nparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8009\ncomponent: MLflow\nteam: MLflow Team\ntype: Implementation\npriority: P0\ndependencies:\n- RHAISTRAT-8009-E004\nai_signals:\n  change_specificity: 1\n  pattern_precedent: 1\n  adapter_pattern: 1\n  existing_foundation: 1\n  open_questions: 0\n  external_dependency: 0\n  human_process_gates: 0\n  repo_access: 1\n  architecture_claims: 1\nimplementation_type: null\ninvestigation_signals: null\nai_implementability: High\nai_implementability_score: 6\njira_key: null\nbranch: null\ngated_by: null\ngate_failure_impact: null\n---\n# Workspace-aware artifacts-only serving in the MLflow tracking server\n\n## Description\n\nMake the MLflow tracking server's `--artifacts-only` mode composable with `--enable-workspaces` so that artifact serving is validated against workspace membership in multi-tenant deployments. When both flags are set, the server requires an explicit `--workspace-store-uri`, initializes only the workspace provider needed to validate artifact requests, and leaves tracking stores, model registry stores, and non-artifact API endpoints uninitialized and disabled. Per Staff Engineer input, no new server flag is introduced — the workspace-aware behavior emerges from combining the two existing flags, and no reference to a separate workspace-aware artifact flag or CR field may be reintroduced.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Compose `--artifacts-only` with `--enable-workspaces`: when both are set, require an explicit `--workspace-store-uri`; fail to start with a clear error if it is missing\n- Initialize only the workspace provider in artifacts-only mode; keep tracking and model registry stores uninitialized and non-artifact API endpoints disabled\n- Add workspace validation to artifact request handling: resolve the artifact path to its parent experiment (artifacts are stored under experiment-scoped paths, e.g. `{experiment_id}/{run_id}/artifacts/`), look up the experiment's workspace assignment via the workspace store, and delegate authorization to the existing auth plugin infrastructure — without dictating Kubernetes API mechanics inside the plugin\n- Deny requests from users without workspace membership with HTTP 403 and an error message indicating insufficient workspace permissions\n- Fail closed: if the workspace store is unreachable while workspace-aware mode is active, deny artifact requests (HTTP 503 indicating workspace validation is unavailable) rather than serving without validation\n- Preserve current behavior when either flag is absent: `--artifacts-only` without `--enable-workspaces` serves artifacts without workspace validation, and deployments that do not set workspace flags are unaffected\n- Implement short-TTL caching of workspace membership decisions, consistent with caching patterns already used in the tracking server's auth path, to bound the per-request latency of workspace resolution\n- Scope initial artifact path resolution to the S3 artifact store (the standard RHOAI configuration, where paths follow the `{experiment_id}/{run_id}/artifacts/` structure) and document limitations for non-standard artifact roots\n- Measure and report the per-request latency overhead added by workspace validation\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- Given the artifacts-only instance started with `--artifacts-only --enable-workspaces --workspace-store-uri kubernetes://` and a user who is a member of workspace A, when the user requests an artifact belonging to an experiment in workspace A, then the artifact is served — measured by HTTP 200 with correct artifact content\n- Given the same deployment and a user who is a member of workspace A but not workspace B, when the user requests an artifact belonging to an experiment in workspace B, then the request is denied — measured by HTTP 403 with an error message indicating insufficient workspace permissions\n- Given `--artifacts-only` set without `--enable-workspaces`, when any authenticated user requests an artifact, then it is served without workspace validation — measured by HTTP 200 regardless of workspace membership (current behavior preserved)\n- Given workspace-aware mode active and the workspace store (Kubernetes API) unreachable, when a user requests an artifact, then the request is denied — measured by HTTP 503 indicating workspace validation is unavailable (fail-closed)\n- Given `--enable-workspaces` set with `--artifacts-only` but no `--workspace-store-uri`, the server fails to start with a clear error\n- Single-tenant deployments and configurations without workspace flags retain current behavior, covered by regression tests\n- The per-request latency overhead of workspace validation is measured and reported unconditionally; once PM/Engineering defines a latency budget (open question OQ1, pending), the measured overhead is compared against that budget and the comparison included in the report\n- Limitations of artifact path resolution for non-standard artifact roots are documented\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- [P0] `--artifacts-only` can be combined with `--enable-workspaces` and `--workspace-store-uri` to enable workspace-validated artifact serving\n- [P0] Artifact requests through the workspace-aware artifacts-only server are validated against workspace membership; requests from users without workspace membership are denied with HTTP 403\n- [P0] Workspace-aware artifact serving is fail-closed: if the workspace store is unreachable, requests are denied\n- [P0] Single-tenant deployments and configurations without `--enable-workspaces` retain current behavior with no changes\n---\nepic_id: RHAISTRAT-8009-E004\ntitle: Artifact-context workspace resolution in the MLflow Kubernetes plugins\nparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8009\ncomponent: MLflow\nteam: MLflow Team\ntype: Implementation\npriority: P0\ndependencies:\n- RHAISTRAT-8009-E001\ngated_by: RHAISTRAT-8009-E001\ngate_failure_impact:\n  action: rewrite\n  fallback_approach: If the investigation finds workspace resolution is not coupled\n    to tracking store initialization, this epic reduces to verification and test coverage\n    in the artifacts-only context; if coupled, it includes refactoring workspace resolution\n    into an independent initialization path within the plugin.\nai_signals:\n  change_specificity: 0\n  pattern_precedent: 1\n  adapter_pattern: 1\n  existing_foundation: 1\n  open_questions: -1\n  external_dependency: 0\n  human_process_gates: 0\n  repo_access: 0\n  architecture_claims: 1\nimplementation_type: null\ninvestigation_signals: null\nai_implementability: High\nai_implementability_score: 3\njira_key: null\nbranch: null\n---\n# Artifact-context workspace resolution in the MLflow Kubernetes plugins\n\n## Description\n\nVerify and extend the mlflow-kubernetes-plugins auth plugin so that workspace resolution — the experiment-to-workspace mapping provided by the workspace provider registered through the `mlflow.workspace_provider` entry point group — functions correctly in the artifacts-only server context, where tracking stores and model registry stores are not initialized. If the workspace resolution is currently coupled to the full tracking server initialization path, factor it into an independent initialization path within the plugin so the artifacts-only server can call the same workspace resolution logic before serving or proxying artifact requests.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Verify the plugin's workspace resolution works when initialized in the artifacts-only context: workspace store only, no tracking store or model registry store initialization\n- If the investigation into the plugin's initialization dependencies finds coupling to tracking store availability, refactor workspace resolution into an independent initialization path within the plugin\n- Ensure workspace resolution is callable with artifact-request context (the experiment ID resolved from the artifact path) and returns the workspace assignment the server's authorization check needs\n- Keep the kube-rbac-proxy sidecar and `--app-name kubernetes-auth` setup unchanged — authentication at the ingress level works identically for the tracking server and the artifacts-only instance (Staff Engineer input)\n- Add test coverage for workspace resolution in the artifacts-only context, covering member and non-member outcomes\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- Workspace resolution (experiment-to-workspace lookup) is demonstrated to function in a server context with no tracking store or model registry store initialization\n- If coupling to the full server initialization path was found, workspace resolution is factored into an independent initialization path within the plugin and the refactoring is covered by tests\n- Workspace resolution resolves artifact-request context to a workspace assignment with correct member/non-member results in tests\n- No change is required to the kube-rbac-proxy sidecar or `--app-name kubernetes-auth` setup\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- [P1] Auth plugin workspace resolution functions correctly in the artifacts-only context without tracking store initialization\n- Note on priority: the HLR is labeled P1 in the strategy, but this epic is P0 by priority inheritance — workspace resolution enables the P0 membership-validation and fail-closed HLRs delivered by the server-side workspace-aware artifact serving work","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8a9c40e3d009621d","agent_id":"a41998ef9102eb0c4","session_id":"1dcc8d9d-3485-4c95-9a3d-f9307d0024ef","uuid":"5de6d31b-d446-43d7-8226-ffb1978fc125","timestamp":"2026-10-23T18:30:01.236Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-81f03ede6b53af65","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Re-review RHAISTRAT-8009 decomposition"}
