{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9d00289fcdd1f7d7","type":"tool_result","content":"---\nepic_id: RHAISTRAT-8009-E005\ntitle: Split-server artifact serving topology in the MLflow operator\nparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8009\ncomponent: MLflow\nteam: MLflow Team\ntype: Implementation\npriority: P1\ndependencies:\n- RHAISTRAT-8009-E002\ngated_by: RHAISTRAT-8009-E002\ngate_failure_impact:\n  action: rewrite\n  fallback_approach: If the routing design selects gateway-level routing, artifact\n    traffic is routed via HTTPRoute rules with no main tracking deployment change;\n    if the tracking server needs a configuration change to delegate artifact operations,\n    the main deployment configuration and routing templates are rewritten to that\n    design.\nai_signals:\n  change_specificity: 0\n  pattern_precedent: 1\n  adapter_pattern: 1\n  existing_foundation: 1\n  open_questions: -1\n  external_dependency: 0\n  human_process_gates: 0\n  repo_access: 1\n  architecture_claims: 1\nimplementation_type: null\ninvestigation_signals: null\nai_implementability: High\nai_implementability_score: 4\njira_key: null\nbranch: null\n---\n# Split-server artifact serving topology in the MLflow operator\n\n## Description\n\nExtend the MLflow operator to deploy a dedicated artifacts-only MLflow instance as a separate Deployment with its own Service and HTTPRoute, and to route artifact traffic from the main tracking deployment to that dedicated instance — a split-server topology, not a single-deployment configuration toggle (Staff Engineer input). The main tracking server continues to handle experiment tracking, model registry, and prompt registry; the artifacts-only instance handles artifact serving with workspace validation. Users configure a single MLflow CR and the operator manages the topology declaratively.\n\n## Scope\n\n- New Helm chart templates within the operator's existing Helm-in-operator pattern (CR spec converted to Helm values, rendered through embedded chart templates, applied via Server-Side Apply) for the artifacts-only Deployment, Service, and HTTPRoute\n- Propagate workspace configuration (`--enable-workspaces`, `--workspace-store-uri`, `--app-name kubernetes-auth`, `--serve-artifacts`) to the artifacts-only instance using the same configuration mechanisms already used for the main tracking deployment, combining `--artifacts-only` with the workspace flags — no new CR field of the form `spec.artifactsOnly.workspaceAware` (per Staff Engineer input, workspace-aware behavior comes from flag composition)\n- Configure artifact traffic routing from the main tracking deployment to the dedicated artifacts-only instance per the selected routing design (gateway-level routing or main-deployment delegation, as determined by the routing design investigation)\n- Create the artifacts-only instance's Gateway API HTTPRoute conditionally based on runtime CRD discovery, consistent with the operator's existing pattern for optional dependencies\n- Operator validation: reject configurations where the artifacts-only instance would run without the workspace flags when workspace-aware artifact serving is intended, so misconfiguration cannot silently bypass workspace validation\n- Deploy the kube-rbac-proxy sidecar on the artifacts-only instance identically to the main tracking deployment\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- Given an MLflow CR with split-server artifact serving enabled, when the operator reconciles the CR, it creates a dedicated artifacts-only Deployment, Service, and HTTPRoute separate from the main tracking Deployment — measured by the presence of distinct Deployment resources with the correct flag combinations\n- The dedicated artifacts-only Deployment runs with `--artifacts-only --enable-workspaces --workspace-store-uri kubernetes:// --app-name kubernetes-auth --serve-artifacts` — measured by inspecting the container command in the rendered Deployment spec\n- Artifact traffic from the main tracking deployment is routed to the dedicated artifacts-only instance per the selected routing design\n- The artifacts-only instance's HTTPRoute is created only when Gateway API CRDs are discovered at runtime (conditional dependency preserved)\n- The operator rejects configurations where the artifacts-only instance would lack workspace flags while workspace-aware artifact serving is intended\n- Deployments that do not enable the split topology reconcile unchanged (backwards compatibility)\n- The rendered topology is verified against a workspace-aware artifacts-only server build (a server image supporting the combined flags) — an acceptance gate on this epic, not a build dependency on the server work\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- [P1] MLflow operator deploys a dedicated artifacts-only instance as a separate Deployment with its own Service and HTTPRoute\n- [P1] Main tracking deployment routes artifact traffic to the dedicated artifacts-only instance\n---\nepic_id: RHAISTRAT-8009-E006\ntitle: Decide the audit telemetry channel for workspace access denials\nparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8009\ncomponent: MLflow\nteam: MLflow Team\ntype: Investigation\npriority: P2\ndependencies: []\ninvestigation_signals:\n  question_specificity: 1\n  source_accessibility: 0\n  local_runnability: 0\n  cluster_hardware_dependence: 0\n  human_judgment_required: -2\nimplementation_type: null\nai_signals: null\nai_implementability: Low\nai_implementability_score: -1\njira_key: null\nbranch: null\ngated_by: null\ngate_failure_impact: null\n---\n# Decide the audit telemetry channel for workspace access denials\n\n## Description\n\nDecide through which telemetry channel workspace access denials in artifact serving should generate audit events: server logs (the baseline stated in the strategy's P2 requirement), OTLP telemetry, or the Kubernetes audit log. The strategy leaves this question to PM/Security with technical feasibility input from the MLflow team; the answer determines the scope of the audit logging implementation. This is a decision investigation: the gating question is a governance decision the MLflow team cannot make alone.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Frame the decision: auditability requirements for workspace access denials in multi-tenant artifact serving — who consumes the events, retention needs, and correlation with other security events\n- Assess the feasibility and cost of each candidate channel: server log emission, OTLP export through the existing observability stack, and Kubernetes audit log integration\n- Record the PM/Security decision with rationale, including whether server logs alone satisfy the requirement or an additional channel is required\n- Specify the audit event content required (identity, workspace, experiment, decision) at the level the audit logging implementation can build against\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- A documented decision selects the audit channel(s) for workspace access denials in artifact serving, with PM/Security sign-off recorded\n- The decision specifies the audit event content and the channel(s) the implementation must emit to\n- If the decision extends beyond server logs, the additional integration scope is identified so the audit logging work can be scoped from it\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- Resolves the strategy's open question \"Should workspace access denials generate audit events through a specific telemetry channel (OTLP, Kubernetes audit log, server log)?\"\n- Gates the [P2] HLR \"Audit logging for workspace access denials in artifact serving through server logs\" — the channel choice determines what the audit implementation builds\n---\nepic_id: RHAISTRAT-8009-E007\ntitle: Audit logging for workspace access denials in artifact serving\nparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8009\ncomponent: MLflow\nteam: MLflow Team\ntype: Implementation\npriority: P2\ndependencies:\n- RHAISTRAT-8009-E003\n- RHAISTRAT-8009-E006\ngated_by: RHAISTRAT-8009-E006\ngate_failure_impact:\n  action: rewrite\n  fallback_approach: If the channel decision selects server logs (the HLR baseline),\n    the implementation emits structured denial records to server logs only; if OTLP\n    or the Kubernetes audit log is selected, the emission layer is rewritten to the\n    chosen channel(s).\nai_signals:\n  change_specificity: 0\n  pattern_precedent: 1\n  adapter_pattern: 0\n  existing_foundation: 1\n  open_questions: -1\n  external_dependency: 0\n  human_process_gates: 0\n  repo_access: 1\n  architecture_claims: 1\nimplementation_type: null\ninvestigation_signals: null\nai_implementability: High\nai_implementability_score: 3\njira_key: null\nbranch: null\n---\n# Audit logging for workspace access denials in artifact serving\n\n## Description\n\nImplement audit event emission for workspace access denials in the workspace-aware artifacts-only server, through the telemetry channel selected by the audit channel decision (baseline per the strategy requirement: server logs). This instruments the workspace-denial code path created by the workspace-aware artifact serving capability: both membership denials (HTTP 403) and fail-closed denials (HTTP 503) must be auditable.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Emit an audit record for each workspace access denial in artifact serving, containing the requesting identity, the workspace, the experiment, and the decision\n- Implement emission through the selected channel — server logs by default, plus any additional channel the audit channel decision requires\n- Emit denial audit events at a severity consistent with security-relevant events in the server's existing logging conventions\n- Add test coverage: denial events are emitted for both membership denials and fail-closed denials\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- Every workspace access denial in artifact serving (HTTP 403 membership denial and HTTP 503 fail-closed denial) produces an audit record through the selected channel\n- Audit records contain the requesting identity, workspace, experiment, and decision\n- Denial audit emission is covered by tests for both denial modes\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- [P2] Audit logging for workspace access denials in artifact serving through server logs (channel per the audit channel decision)\n- Note on split: this work is separated from the P0 server implementation because the audit channel is an open question whose answer changes what gets built; it instruments the denial path the server implementation creates rather than building a second surface\n---\nepic_id: RHAISTRAT-8009-E008\ntitle: End-to-end integration validation of workspace-aware artifact serving\nparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8009\ncomponent: MLflow\nteam: MLflow Team\ntype: Implementation\npriority: P1\ndependencies:\n- RHAISTRAT-8009-E003\n- RHAISTRAT-8009-E004\n- RHAISTRAT-8009-E005\nai_signals:\n  change_specificity: 1\n  pattern_precedent: 1\n  adapter_pattern: 0\n  existing_foundation: 1\n  open_questions: 0\n  external_dependency: 0\n  human_process_gates: 0\n  repo_access: 1\n  architecture_claims: 1\nimplementation_type: null\ninvestigation_signals: null\nai_implementability: High\nai_implementability_score: 5\njira_key: null\nbranch: null\ngated_by: null\ngate_failure_impact: null\n---\n# End-to-end integration validation of workspace-aware artifact serving\n\n## Description\n\nValidate the assembled workspace-aware artifact serving capability end to end across the split-server topology: the workspace-aware artifacts-only server, the auth plugin's workspace resolution in the artifacts-only context, and the operator-deployed dedicated instance with artifact traffic routing from the main tracking deployment. This is the strategy's dedicated integration testing effort — multi-tenant workspace scenarios, backwards compatibility, single-tenant regression, fail-closed edge cases, and split-server routing.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Multi-tenant workspace scenarios through the operator-deployed topology: member of the owning workspace is served (HTTP 200, correct content); non-member is denied (HTTP 403)\n- Fail-closed edge cases: workspace store (Kubernetes API) unreachable yields denials (HTTP 503), never silent degradation of tenant isolation\n- Single-tenant regression and backwards compatibility: deployments without workspace flags, and artifacts-only mode without `--enable-workspaces`, retain current behavior\n- Split-server routing: artifact traffic from the main tracking deployment reaches the dedicated artifacts-only instance; operator validation rejects misconfigured topologies (artifacts-only instance lacking workspace flags)\n- Artifact path resolution end to end on the S3 artifact store (the standard RHOAI configuration, `{experiment_id}/{run_id}/artifacts/` structure)\n- Capture test results and evidence mapping each strategy acceptance criterion to its observed result\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- All multi-tenant workspace scenarios pass through the operator-deployed split topology (member served with correct content; non-member denied with HTTP 403)\n- Fail-closed behavior is verified: workspace store unreachability produces denials (HTTP 503), not unvalidated serves\n- Single-tenant and no-workspace-flag regression scenarios pass (current behavior preserved)\n- Artifact traffic routing between the main tracking deployment and the dedicated artifacts-only instance is verified end to end\n- A validation report maps each strategy acceptance criterion to its observed result\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- Validation coverage for [P0] workspace-validated artifact serving, [P0] membership denial (HTTP 403), [P0] fail-closed behavior, [P0] backwards compatibility, [P1] dedicated artifacts-only instance deployment, and [P1] artifact traffic routing — the implementing epics own these HLRs; this epic validates them end to end","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8a9c40e3d009621d","agent_id":"a41998ef9102eb0c4","session_id":"1dcc8d9d-3485-4c95-9a3d-f9307d0024ef","uuid":"5b6615e4-90d5-4139-878c-481e83caee3c","timestamp":"2026-10-23T18:30:01.649Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"c